No tactics are shouting for attention; the pieces are settling in for a long positional argument.

White's pawn structure is the happier one — the opponent's carries scars that will not heal by themselves. Black's d5-pawn is isolated. Meanwhile, White's minor pieces have found real work; their counterparts are still waiting for a career. White owns the bishop pair in an open position.

Here is what White should be dreaming about: park a piece in front of the weak pawn so it can never move again, then pile on it at your leisure (key squares: d4 and d5). Another dream for White: look for the break that throws the position open.
